    "durationMs": 372902,
    "finalText": "The URL shortener is complete and fully tested. What I built:\n\n- **`src/main.rs`** — Axum 0.8.4 API on a SQLx PostgreSQL pool. POST /links validates URLs (absolute http/https with host, via the `url` crate) and codes (4–20 ASCII letters/digits/hyphens/underscores), generates 8-char codes when omitted, returns 409 on duplicate custom codes (detected via Postgres `23505`), and echoes the submitted URL verbatim. A custom `JsonBody` extractor maps every JSON rejection (malformed body, missing/invalid fields) to 422 with an `error` field. GET /r/{code} redirects with 302 + `Location` and increments clicks atomically in one `UPDATE ... RETURNING` (exactly once per request). GET /links lists in `id` order with `total`. Unknown codes → 404, malformed path codes → 422.\n- **`migrations/0001_create_links.sql`** — `links` table (`code` unique, `url`, `clicks` counter), applied at startup by the embedded `sqlx::migrate!` before the listener binds.\n- **`start.sh`** — executable; runs the prebuilt release binary (rebuilds only if missing) in the foreground on `0.0.0.0:8000`, with `DATABASE_URL` read from the environment.\n- **`README.md`** — documents startup via `sh start.sh`.\n\nI verified end-to-end against the running Postgres: create/duplicate/422 validation cases, redirect click counting (1 → 3 across repeated hits), list ordering, and a full server restart showing data and click counts persist. The test server is stopped and port 8000 is free."
  }
